-- title:
--   No selected ternary probe exactly characterizes an ordinary fibre
-- statement:
--   Let $d\in\mathbb N$, let $q_0,\ldots,q_{d-1}$ be positive integers, and put $G=\prod_{j<d}\mathbb Z/q_j\mathbb Z$. Let $S$ be a three-element support with square equal to the function represented by typed entries. Then
--
--   $$\operatorname{TernaryChoice}(\mathrm{entries})=\mathrm{none}\Longleftrightarrow |\mathcal F(S)|=|G[2]|.$$
--
--   This identifies exactly when the binary bank suffices on the represented fibre.
-- source:
--   Alexandre Couret, Couret — Additive reconstruction: STROBO-4, singleton collisions and RECOH formal proof artifacts, https://zenodo.org/records/23980513; archive COURET_MATHEMATIQUES_20261105.zip, SHA-256 19a3b59aee930df74cab79c27b29e014843bd17179a26fef1261ed6b5a651ab7; file RECOH/lean/RecohSquareSelector.lean, lines 393–429; source file SHA-256 a8eaaafaf12bee0781361925c6726f9d658ee4df71e8e3c8436c68366ccc7438; theorem Couret.STROBO2TORS.recoh_square_ternary_choice_none_iff

